"title": "Beware of flames and dizziness",
"body": "Ethylene is an extremely flammable gas that can cause drowsiness or dizziness. It should be kept away from flames and handled with care.",
"sources": ["s1"]
}

{
"title": "Ancient Dye with a Rich History",
"body": "Indigo dye, dating back 6,000 years to Huaca Prieta, Peru, was used by ancient civilizations across Mesopotamia, Egypt, Britain, Mesoamerica, and West Africa. India, the earliest major center for indigo production, domesticated Indigofera tinctoria and exported the dye to Greece, Rome, and eventually Europe. The term 'indigo' originates from the Greek 'indikón' (Ἰνδικόν), reflecting India's association with the dye.",
"sources": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
}

{
"title": "Beware of explosions and toxic fumes",
"body": "Nitroglycerin is officially rated as an explosive, toxic if swallowed, fatal upon skin contact, and fatal if inhaled. It may cause organ damage with prolonged exposure and is toxic to aquatic life with long-lasting effects.",
"sources": ["s1"]
}

{
"title": "Watch out for your eyes and skin!", 
"body": "Quinine is officially rated as a danger to your eyes and skin. It can cause serious eye irritation and skin irritation. If inhaled, it may cause allergy or asthma symptoms or breathing difficulties.", 
"sources": ["s1"]
}

{
"title": "A sweet taste on his hands",
"body": "Quinine was used as a muscle relaxant by the Quechua Indians of Peru, Bolivia and Ecuador to halt shivering. The Spanish Jesuit missionaries were the first to bring cinchona to Europe. A popular story of how it was brought to Europe by the Countess of Chinchon was disproven by medical historian Alec Haggis around 1941.",
"sources": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4"]
}

{
"title": "Discovery of a Vasoconstrictor Material",
"body": "In 1935, Vittorio Erspamer discovered a vasoconstrictor material in serum, initially believed to contain adrenaline. In 1948, Maurice M. Rapport and colleagues identified it as serotonin. By 1952, enteramine was confirmed as serotonin, leading to its widespread use in pharmacology. Synonyms include 5-hydroxytriptamine and enteramine.",
"sources": ["s1", "s2"]
}

{
"title": "Vanillin's ancient origins",
"body": "Vanillin was found in jars from Canaan dating back to the 2nd millennium BCE, suggesting ancient cultivation of vanilla-like plants. This predates the domestication of vanilla in Mesoamerica by centuries!",
"sources": ["s1", "s2"]
}
